The issue is in the `IOUtils.skip` method, shown below:

```java
public static long skip(InputStream input, long numToSkip) throws IOException {
    long available = numToSkip;
    while (numToSkip > 0) {
        long skipped = input.skip(numToSkip);
        if (skipped == 0) {
            break;
        }
        numToSkip -= skipped;
    }

    return available - numToSkip;
}
```

The failing test `IOUtilsTest.skipUsingRead()` provides a custom implementation of `InputStream` that always returns 0 when `skip(long)` is called:

```java
@Test
public void skipUsingRead() throws Exception {
    skip(new StreamWrapper() {
        public InputStream wrap(InputStream toWrap) {
            return new FilterInputStream(toWrap) {
                public long skip(long s) {
                    return 0;
                }
            };
        }
    });
}
```

To fix this issue, the implementation of `IOUtils.skip(InputStream, long)` should be revised to handle the case where `input.skip(numToSkip)` always returns 0 appropriately.

The root cause of the problem lies in the fact that the `skip` method in the custom `FilterInputStream` always returns 0, causing the loop in `IOUtils.skip` to continue indefinitely. Therefore, the fix should include ensuring that the `skip` method in the custom `FilterInputStream` behaves as expected.

The potential locations to fix this issue are:
```
org.apache.commons.compress.utils.IOUtils.skip(InputStream, long)
```

